{"id":"R0097","study_type":"Empirical analysis; fully automated LLM-driven prototype and Linux privilege-escalation benchmark","RQ1 task_stage":"Autonomously enumerate and execute Linux privilege-escalation attacks; gain root-level access from a low-privilege account","RQ1 domain":"Linux systems; local vulnerable Debian GNU/Linux virtual machines","RQ1 orientation":"Offensive security; penetration-testing","RQ2 architecture":"Single-LLM control loop; optional second call for state-compaction/reflection, truncated command history, guidance and SSH tools for executing commands and testing credentials","RQ2 llm_used":"GPT-3.5-Turbo; GPT-4-Turbo; Llama3-70b (4-bit quantized) and Llama3-8b (8-bit quantized)","RQ2 adaptation":"Prompting; optional high-level or enumeration-derived hints, LLM-driven reflection/state-compaction, and in-context learning with selected hacktricks articles","RQ3 autonomy_level":"All decision-making is performed by LLMs through prompting; automatically generates and executes commands until root access or the configured round limit","RQ3 hitl":"Human penetration-tester triggers execution; optional high-level hints can be supplied by a human penetration-tester","RQ4 dataset":"Newly curated Linux privilege-escalation benchmark of single-vulnerability virtual machines; captured execution trajectories","RQ4 environment":"Local Debian GNU/Linux VMs provisioned with Vagrant and Ansible; fresh VMs created and destroyed for each benchmark run","RQ4 metrics":"Successful exploitation/root access, number of rounds, elapsed time, token counts and monetary costs; qualitative command quality and operational behaviors","RQ4 baseline_type":"Human professional penetration-tester; traditional automated privilege-escalation tools","RQ4 headline_result":"GPT-4-Turbo exploited 33–83% of vulnerabilities, compared with 75% for the human baseline; reflection doubled unaided GPT-4-Turbo success from 33% to 66%","RQ4 compared_to":"Human penetration-tester; human with hints; traitor; pwncat-cs","RQ4 code_available":"yes + https://github.com/ipa-lab/hackingBuddyGPT","RQ4 data_available":"yes + https://github.com/ipa-lab/benchmark-privesc-linux ; https://github.com/ipa-lab/hackingbuddy-results","RQ5 risks_addressed":"Potentially destructive experiments, system instability and data loss; interaction with non-test systems/networks, host compromise and benchmark contamination","RQ5 mitigations":"Local, non-publicly reachable virtual networks and air-gap-capable VMs with no shared host resources; fresh VMs per run, round limits and custom benchmark cases","author_limitations (verbatim)":"not stated","author_future_work (verbatim)":"If future LLM-driven prototypes are capable of exploiting more testbeds, additional test-cases, e.g., multi-step test-cases utilizing multiple user-accounts, can be incorporated.","evidence (page/section)":"Abstract, pp. 1–2: results and reliability challenges; §§1.3, 2.4: contributions, release URLs and architecture; §§2.2–2.2.3: tasks and safety risks; §§3.1–3.3: environment, baselines, autonomy, human involvement and tools; §§3.4–3.6: models, prompting, context management and metrics; §§4.1–4.1.1: safety mitigations and future work. Supplied text ends during §4.2, p. 21."}
END_JSON
